{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1786210152,"nonce":"yB56BS/txqxss72okN7vTGQm/Ng=","target":"c1d05a7d8acaeab65069ab1fb47aaa86c99c286d1e04be1e5c3d7c1ff197f7b5","message":"**What is wrong.** `GET /api/v1/alerts?include=rule` returns only the snapshot's\nid. `internal/alerts/api/handlers.go:108-112` states it deliberately: \"⛔\n`include=rule` carries the snapshot ID and nothing more. The full\n`RuleSnapshotDTO` is owned by `rules/api` — CONTEXT.md §5.4 forbids this package\nfrom naming `rules/domain` — and `/alerts/{id}/rule` serves it whole.\" The\ncontract was amended to match (commit `3870be3`), so this is no longer a\nconformance defect — it is a capability the list view does not have.\n\n**Why it matters.** \"*what the rule said at that moment*\" is the first promise in\n`README.md` and the point of ADR 0009. The `include=` parameter exists to avoid\nN+1s, and this is the one sub-resource for which it does not work: a list of 50\nalerts showing `expr` or `for` costs 50 extra requests. Either the alert list\nnever shows the rule — losing the differentiator on the screen users spend their\ntime in — or the UI does the N+1 and the list gets slow exactly when there are\nmany alerts.\n\n**Where.** `internal/alerts/api/handlers.go:105-118`, `internal/rules/api/`,\n`CONTEXT.md` §5.4, ADR 0009, `api/openapi/openapi.yaml` (`RuleSnapshotDTO`).\n\n**Done when.** The list view can render the rule without a request per alert —\neither by a batch endpoint under `rules/api` that takes snapshot ids, or by a\nsanctioned way for the alerts handler to embed the DTO that does not breach §5.4 —\nand the chosen shape is recorded so the next reader does not re-litigate the\nlayering rule.","files":null}]}